For an example of how to generate new message types, consider the following
project tree:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATION_PROJECT_TREE@
```

The top-level `CMakeLists.txt` file contains the following:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATION_TOP_LEVEL_CMAKELSTS_TXT@
```

To generate a message, you need to write a `.idl` file. A simple example is
shown in `bool.idl` here:

```idl
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATiON_BOOL_IDL@
```

This `.idl` file needs to be converted into a source and header file. This is
done in `msg/CMakeLists.txt`:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATION_MSG_CMAKELISTS_TXT@
```

A simple way this message can be used is shown in the file
`src/example_message_generation.cpp`:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATiON_SRC_EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATION_CPP@
```

To compile it, you need to tell CMake where the generated header files for the
new messages are. You also need to link the executable to the newly generated
message library. This is shown inside of `src/CMakeLists.txt`:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_MESSAGE_GENERATION_SRC_CMAKELISTS_TXT@
```
